The representation of tier 2 micro-ops is as follows:
```
typedef struct {
    uint32_t opcode;
    uint32_t oparg;
    uint64_t operand;  // A cache entry
} _PyUOpInstruction;
```
which is 16 bytes per micro-op (not so "micro").
 
This is fine for an ephemeral IR in a buffer, but uses too much memory and is too slow for interpretation.

Tier 1 instructions are formed of 1 or more 16 bit "code unit"s. 
We should do the same for tier 2, although it probably makes sense to use 32 bit code units:
```
typedef union {
    uint32_t operand;
    struct {
        uint16_t code;
        uint16_t arg;
    } op;
} _PyUopCodeUnit;
```
An instruction with just an oparg or a 16 bit cache entry will fit into a single code unit. Other instructions will fit into 2 or 3.
This should reduce the size of the code to 30-40% of its current size. With fewer memory loads and less cache pressure, we should expect a decent speedup as well.


